I’ll start by reading the measurement context and the frozen PXRD workflow, then inspect peak positions/intensities and candidate databases before building the GSAS-II model. I’ll keep the phase inventory reversible and test plausible alternates rather than treating a weak first fit as a chemical rejection.

The chemistry is a 1:1 CaCO₃/WO₃ firing at 500 °C, so the initial inventory is CaWO₄ plus possible residual CaCO₃/WO₃ and decomposition products—not a single-phase assumption. The scan has a high low-angle background and full Cu Kα doublet data; I’m now separating coherent peaks from that background and checking exact and prototype structures in the permitted COD snapshot.

The peak families already resolve into three chemically coherent components: monoclinic WO₃ (the sharp 23.42/23.90/24.67° triplet and related peaks), scheelite CaWO₄ (broader 29.02/47.52/56.19° families), and residual calcite CaCO₃ (sharp 29.71° plus 39.71/43.46/48.53°). I’m comparing all physical COD records for those polymorphs now, including alternate WO₃ and CaCO₃ forms, before any scale/cell refinement.

The record screen rejects the high-pressure CaWO₄ polymorph and defective alternate scheelite entry. The retained parents are room-temperature scheelite COD 7219229, room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ COD 1528915, and room-temperature calcite COD 1547347; their calculated line positions reproduce the observed families under one common displacement-like shift. I’m moving these into a neutral GSAS-II baseline now, with size/strain/texture disabled initially and Poisson weights retained.

The first common-profile trial was intentionally rejected: U and X ran to their upper bounds while W ran to its lower bound, a classic attempt to average genuinely phase-dependent widths. I’m returning to the pre-profile baseline and will compare size-only and strain-only broadening for each phase with the common U/W/X held at an interior physical starting point, then re-establish the shared profile after those distinctions are made.

The width trials are now physically interpretable: CaWO₄ requires strong size broadening (strain alone hit its bound), WO₃ is predominantly strain-broadened with a smaller finite-size contribution, and calcite supports moderate strain plus size. I’m reconverging those terms together; this joint step is slower because the cells, scales, and background are being replayed on every cycle.

The combined profile fit reaches Rwp ≈10.38%, but the residual is now dominated by intensity—not position—errors, especially within the resolved WO₃ triplet. That is exactly where the coarse 1960 WO₃ coordinates and the generator’s expected small reference-coordinate mismatch matter. I’m testing symmetry-constrained coordinate refinement phase by phase next, while pruning the unsupported calcite size term and the bound U term.

The unconstrained old WO₃ coordinates improved Rwp but moved some oxygen sites by up to ~1.5 Å, so I’m not accepting that model as physical. The alternate-record test is therefore decisive: room-temperature neutron P2₁/c WO₃ and triclinic P‑1 WO₃ are being fitted under exactly the same restrained background/profile model as the old P2₁/n record.

The alternate-record test resolves the WO₃ ambiguity: the room-temperature neutron P2₁/c record (COD 2311041) gives Rwp 8.23%, versus 10.42% for the coarse P2₁/n record and 8.40% for triclinic P‑1, under the same nuisance model. Its peak-family intensities also explain the nearly equal observed 23.42/23.90/24.67° triplet, so I’m adopting it and will only allow modest symmetry-constrained coordinate adjustments from that physical parent.

The common intensity screen finds no supported CaWO₄ texture and only near-unity WO₃ texture; calcite shows a small, plausible [001] March–Dollase effect (ratio ≈0.92), but I’m keeping it provisional because alternate calcite axes are nearly degenerate. Before accepting any texture, I’m checking whether the remaining family-dependent residuals are actually anisotropic broadening, which is the cleaner explanation when peak widths—not integrated areas—differ.

The normalized source check matters here: the frozen generator does not use COD displacement tensors directly; it rewrites each registered structure with neutral elements and Uiso = 0.005 Å². I’ve reproduced that documented normalization for the three retained parents and am rebuilding the accepted P2₁/c/anisotropic-width model from those exact conventions before judging coordinates or fractions.

The rebuilt model uses normalized source structures (all starting Uiso = 0.005 Å²) and the accepted WO₃ [010] uniaxial strain. This replay is still reconverging; once it finishes, I’ll test only bounded coordinate shifts consistent with the documented 0.01–0.05 Å reference mismatch, which prevents the earlier unphysical oxygen drift.

The normalized Uiso screen shows CaWO₄ is already optimal near 0.005 Å²; WO₃ prefers the physical lower edge (~0.003 Å²); calcite tries to exceed the allowed laboratory range, so its ADP refinement is acting as an intensity surrogate and is rejected for now. Bounded coordinate adjustments are small and replay-stable; I’m combining them, then I’ll separate calcite ADP from its weak [001] texture in a direct comparison.

The three-phase model is now converged at about 7.19% Rwp. I’m inspecting the residual for coherent unmodeled peak families, then I’ll freeze the supported parameters and generate the exact replayable deliverables from that same project.

The residual’s largest positive lobes mostly coincide with reflections already present, but a few shoulders near 34.14° and 41.96–42.35° deserve an explicit inventory challenge. I’m testing chemically plausible Ca–W–O/CaO alternatives under the same fixed nuisance model; a phase will be retained only if it develops a stable positive scale and explains several distinct lines.

The watch-list phases all refined to zero or sub‑0.3% unstable contributions and failed their unique-line checks; replacement tests for reduced tungsten oxides worsened the fit from 7.19% to roughly 49% Rwp. I’m keeping the three-phase inventory. One final structural check remains: coordinate perturbations at the documented 0.01–0.05 Å scale, especially the strongly scattering W sites, to avoid both under-refinement and nonphysical intensity surrogates.

An important inventory result emerged: a second room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ record develops a reproducible ~9 wt% scale and lowers Rwp by ~0.21 points, while the triclinic alternate gives only ~2 wt% and a smaller gain. I’m letting the cell/width replay finish, then I’ll test whether the monoclinic pair has genuinely distinct peak families or is merely using two nearly identical structures as an intensity/profile surrogate.

The second WO₃ component passes the stronger test: fixing a single WO₃ phase and allowing a nine-parameter Stephens strain model reaches 6.16% Rwp, whereas the two-record model reaches 5.99% with comparable parameter count and physically separate broad/sharp line families. I’m therefore retaining it provisionally as a fourth crystalline component, while treating “two monoclinic WO₃ phases vs. one phase with a complex domain/strain distribution” as the main remaining ambiguity.

The four-component model is now physically clean at 5.85% Rwp: the sharp WO₃ component supports [100] uniaxial strain, weak [111] texture, Uiso at 0.003 Å², and coordinate shifts that remain within the stated 0.04/0.05 Å element-wise RMS limits. I’m doing the required post-inventory coordinate revisit on the other three phases, then I’ll lock the model and export/replay it.

The structural inventory is now narrowed to CaWO₄, residual CaCO₃, and two distinguishable monoclinic WO₃ profile components. I’m finishing the texture re-challenge and exact GSAS-II replay/export now; the key remaining judgment is how explicitly to qualify the two-WO₃ split, since it may represent domain populations rather than two thermodynamically distinct phases.

Both preferred-orientation terms survive the final challenge: removing calcite [001] worsens Rwp from 5.790% to 5.850%, and removing the sharper WO₃ component’s [111] term worsens it to 5.879%. The sharper WO₃ crystallite-size term did not survive testing, so I have neutralized it; that avoids using peak-width nuisance parameters to manufacture the split.

The final project now refines all 8,293 supplied points and is save/reopen stable: Rwp varies only 0.00003 percentage point on replay, with no frozen or rejected parameters. Its crystalline fractions settle at 12.18% CaWO₄, 31.47% CaCO₃, 40.65% broad WO₃, and 15.70% sharper WO₃. I’m now checking atomic geometry and project/CIF/profile consistency before writing the deliverables.

One geometry check caught a real problem in the sharper WO₃ coordinate trial: its last bounded oxygen-coordinate adjustment creates a 1.577 Å O–O contact. I’m rejecting that coordinate realization and re-testing the source geometry plus tighter physically screened coordinate variants. This should leave the inventory intact, but the exact split fractions and Rwp may shift slightly.

The physical alternate is decisively better, not merely acceptable: using the well-behaved modern monoclinic WO₃ geometry for both domain populations drops Rwp from 5.79% to about 5.31%. The first all-at-once refinement exposed strong scale/profile correlation between those nearly identical components, so I’m switching to staged reconvergence with backtracking; I will retain only a replay-stable minimum.

The stable physical model shows both WO₃ directional-strain differences are statistically insignificant (each axial/equatorial difference is below one combined standard uncertainty), so I’m pruning those two anisotropy parameters. Because GSAS-II can leave a stale plotted array after a rejected Levenberg step, I’m comparing these reduced models with fresh parameter-only forward replays, not the cached profile.

The two accepted texture terms are clearly real under the revised model: removing calcite [001] raises replay Rwp from 5.301% to 5.382%, and removing the sharper WO₃ orientation term raises it to 5.345%. I’m completing the symmetry-distinct axis comparison for that WO₃ term now, then the final project can be frozen and exported.

The final physically screened model is now fixed. Its verified full-grid profile gives Rwp 5.3011% (Rp 4.1419%); the fractions are 11.98% scheelite CaWO₄, 31.85% calcite, and 56.17% monoclinic WO₃ split into 31.49% broad and 24.69% sharp domain populations. I’m running the final independent project/CIF/fraction consistency check and writing the uncertainty note.

Completed and independently replay-verified. Final Rwp is 5.3011%.

Crystalline fractions:

- CaWO₄ scheelite: 11.98 wt.%
- CaCO₃ calcite: 31.85 wt.%
- Monoclinic WO₃, broad component: 31.49 wt.%
- Monoclinic WO₃, sharp component: 24.69 wt.%

The combined WO₃ fraction is 56.17 wt.%. The WO₃ split likely represents two domain/strain populations rather than reliably distinct thermodynamic phases.

